"""Second-pass enrichment: appends paragraphs to famous appellation summaries. Applied at merge time
(merge_na.py imports EXTRA). Kept separate so the base part files stay readable."""
EXTRA = {
 "Willamette Valley": "The valley's story is one of outsiders proving experts wrong. In the 1960s UC Davis advised that Oregon was too cold and wet for vinifera; David Lett, Dick Erath, David Adelsheim and Dick Ponzi planted anyway, choosing hillsides above the frost-prone floor. Today vintages swing between cool, rainy years that give translucent, high-acid Pinot and warm years (2014, 2015, 2018, 2022) that give richer, darker wines. Burgundian investment (Drouhin from 1987, Louis Jadot's Résonance, Lafon's Lingua Franca partnership, Roserock) and Champagne interest in sparkling wine have made the Willamette the New World's closest analogue to the Côte d'Or in spirit, though its soils (volcanic, marine sediment, loess) are quite different.",
 "Mt. Harlan": "Jensen planted his first three Pinot Noir parcels in 1975 at about 670 m, far from any town and with scarce water. Yields are very low because of the thin soils and drought. Calera's Pinots are known for long ageing: they start firm and savoury and open into mushroom, rose and red-fruit perfume after a decade. Jensen sold the winery to Duckhorn in 2017, and the estate continues to bottle each named parcel separately.",
 "Red Mountain": "The sandy, calcareous soils over basalt are low in organic matter and very free-draining, so growers control vigour tightly with drip irrigation; vines are often own-rooted. Afternoon wind keeps the canopy dry and stresses the berries, shrinking them and thickening their skins, which is why the wines carry Washington's firmest tannins. The 2001 AVA was one of the first Washington appellations defined on such a small scale. Col Solare (founded by Antinori and Ste. Michelle), Duckhorn's Canvasback and Quilceda Creek's Galitzine Vineyard show how outside investment followed the reputation.",
 "Paso Robles": "The 2014 division into 11 districts came after a decade of debate and studies by the Paso Robles AVA Committee showing large differences in rainfall (from under 250 mm in the east to over 760 mm in the west), soils and temperature. Paso is also one of California's most ambitious Rhône regions: the Rhône Rangers movement of the 1980s and 1990s found its home here, and Saxum's James Berry Vineyard bottlings have been among the most highly scored American Rhône blends. Water is the key political issue, since the Paso Robles groundwater basin is overdrawn and the county has limited new plantings.",
 "Columbia Valley": "The scale is hard to grasp: Columbia Valley covers about a quarter of Washington State, yet vines occupy only a small fraction, clustered on south-facing slopes and benches near the rivers where cold air drains away in winter. Growing degree days range from cool (Ancient Lakes) to very warm (Wahluke Slope, Red Mountain). Most vineyards are own-rooted on sandy, phylloxera-hostile soils, which lets growers replant quickly after freezes. The AVA also crosses into Oregon around Milton-Freewater and the Columbia Gorge, so some Oregon wineries use the name.",
 "Edna Valley": "Paragon Vineyard, planted by the Niven family from 1973, was the valley's pioneer and still supplies many wineries; the Nivens later founded Baileyana and Tangent, and Edna Valley Vineyard was a joint venture with Chalone. Because the valley points straight at the Pacific through Morro Bay, the afternoon breeze arrives early and the growing season can stretch from February budbreak to November harvest, giving Chardonnay its hallmark combination of ripeness and acidity. Since 2022 Edna Valley has sat inside the San Luis Obispo Coast AVA.",
 "Santa Ynez Valley": "The valley is a textbook of how distance from the ocean controls style. At the western end, Sta. Rita Hills grows taut Pinot Noir and Chardonnay; in the middle, Ballard Canyon and Los Olivos ripen Syrah and Grenache; at the eastern end, Happy Canyon is much warmer on summer afternoons and grows Cabernet. The town of Los Olivos and Solvang, a Danish-themed village, are the tourist hubs, and the 2004 film Sideways was largely shot along this valley's roads.",
 "Monticello": "Charlottesville's wine scene grew from Gabriele Rausse's 1976 plantings at Barboursville and Horton Vineyards' bet on Viognier (from 1991), which led Virginia to adopt Viognier as its signature white. Petit Manseng, whose thick skins resist humidity, has become another local success. Summer rain and hurricanes remain the main threats, so growers favour well-drained slopes in the foothills (around 150 to 450 m), open canopies and early-ripening clones.",
 "Augusta": "Augusta's 19th-century winemakers were mostly German immigrants, many of them liberal refugees of the failed revolutions of 1848. The Missouri River Valley was dubbed the 'Missouri Rhineland'. After Prohibition the industry vanished until Lucian Dressel bought Mount Pleasant in 1966 and replanted. Norton, a native grape that thrives in humid summers, makes deep, inky, high-acid reds that are the region's calling card.",
 "Sonoma Coast": "TTB drew Sonoma Coast in 1987 around a 'marine climate' line, but because it reached far inland to the edge of the Sonoma Valley and Russian River, it included warm sites as well as cold ones. That is why growers on the true coast pushed for Fort Ross-Seaview (2011) and West Sonoma Coast (2022). The best Sonoma Coast wines still come from ridgetops near the ocean and from the cool southern hills around Petaluma, where winds and fog keep sugars low and acidity high.",
 "Niagara Peninsula": "The Niagara Escarpment, a limestone ridge that runs from New York to Wisconsin and is a UNESCO biosphere reserve in Ontario, is the key to the appellation. Air off Lake Ontario hits the escarpment and circulates back, so the benches stay a few degrees warmer on cold winter nights and cooler in summer heat. Even so, deep freezes (as in the polar-vortex winters of 2014 and 2015) can kill buds, so growers bury graft unions, run wind machines and plant hardy varieties. Niagara's best Chardonnay and Riesling are among the finest cool-climate wines in North America.",
 "Okanagan Valley": "The valley's north-south axis and its chain of lakes create a steep climate gradient: the desert floor around Osoyoos is among the hottest vineyard land in Canada, while Lake Country, about 175 km north, is much cooler and suited to Riesling and sparkling wine. Long summer days at 49 to 50 degrees north give extra ripening hours, and cold nights keep acidity. The frosts of 2022 and 2024 forced many growers to rethink varieties, with more cold-hardy clones and the replanting of almost all damaged blocks by 2026.",
 "Lodi": "Lodi's sandy soils matter for more than texture: phylloxera cannot move easily through sand, so many old vineyards were never grafted, and some own-rooted Zinfandel and Cinsault vines are more than 130 years old. Growers here developed the Lodi Rules sustainability standard in 2005, now used outside the region. The style ranges from big, ripe, oaky Zinfandel to lighter, fresher bottlings from young producers who pick earlier.",
 "Walla Walla Valley": "Walla Walla is Washington's wine town par excellence: a small, historic farming town with over 100 wineries and tasting rooms in a valley that also grows its famous sweet onions. The valley shares its AVA with Oregon, and the Oregon side's cobbled Rocks District gives the Syrahs that made the area famous among sommeliers. A severe freeze in January 2024 damaged many vines, and growers continue to experiment with burying canes and frost protection.",
 "Prince Edward County": "Burying vines is labour-intensive: in late autumn, growers prune and lay canes in a trench and cover them with soil, then uncover them in spring. That helps them survive winter lows that can fall below minus 25 degrees Celsius. The County has a few dozen wineries, many of them small and family-run, and its Pinot Noir and Chardonnay have drawn comparisons to Burgundy's cooler villages.",
}

US_OVERVIEW_EXTRA = "The modern map is a patchwork of distinct wine cultures. Napa and Sonoma built a luxury economy around Cabernet and estate tourism; the Central Coast and Oregon became the home of America's Pinot Noir and Chardonnay; Washington's irrigated deserts produce structured Bordeaux varieties and Syrah from own-rooted vines; the Finger Lakes made a name with Riesling; Virginia, Texas and the Midwest built regional industries on Cabernet Franc, Tempranillo, Norton and cold-hardy hybrids bred at the University of Minnesota. Much of the country's volume still comes from huge companies (E. & J. Gallo, The Wine Group, Constellation) blending California fruit, while thousands of small wineries sell directly to visitors and mailing-list customers. Direct-to-consumer shipping, legalised across most states after the Supreme Court's Granholm v. Heald ruling in 2005, transformed the economics of small producers."
